UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 8, Lesson 2: Planning an explanation', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to outcome.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOrder a plan for \"Why bread rises\": yeast gases; warm dough; flour/water mix; conclusion.\n\nFEEDBACK\nMix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ).\n\nCHECK\nRandom section order that ignores causes confuses readers.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan an original explanation with title, four ordered sections, two facts per section and a one-sentence purpose for each section.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to outcome.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan an original explanation with title, four ordered sections, two facts per section and a one-sentence purpose for each section. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers.. Correction: Mix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ).. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Mix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ)"],"question":"Order a plan for \"Why bread rises\": yeast gases; warm dough; flour/water mix; conclusion. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for plan; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of explanation and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers","Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to","Mix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ)"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named explanation and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply plan to new material and name the explanation and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers","Name explanation and explain why it matters only, without linking it to plan"],"question":"Which response best applies plan with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers","Point to exact words in the sentence that show plan and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses plan from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Mix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ)","Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name explanation and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse explanation and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about plan","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of plan on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers.. Correction: Mix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ).. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w08-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise plan.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use plan with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify explanation and explain why it matters.","I can practise steps independently on a new prompt.","I can notice order needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to outcome.","options":["Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTopic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lower 4 conclusion. Order matches the science logic.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder a plan for \"Why bread rises\": yeast gases; warm dough; flour/water mix; conclusion.\n\nFEEDBACK\nMix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ).","explain":"Planning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to outcome.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Logical explanation plan order.","caption":"Sections should follow causes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan order</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sun → light → shadow length</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Topic</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Why shadows change</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Topic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lo Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Topic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lo Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTopic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lower 4 conclusion. Order matches the science logic. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Ordered process plan vs random sections.","caption":"Write a purpose for each section.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bread plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">mix → gas → rise</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Random</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sections ignoring causes</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRandom section order that ignores causes confuses readers. Here is a more productive model: Topic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lower 4 conclusion. Order matches the science logic.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nMix ingredients → yeast produces gas in warm dough → dough rises → conclusion (adjust if your sources differ).","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Random section order that ignores causes confuses readers. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlanning an explanation means ordering steps or causes so a reader can follow why something happens from start to outcome. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 8, Lesson 2: Planning an explanation","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Explanations, news and digital texts","week":8,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nTopic: Why shadows change length. Plan: 1 sun position 2 light travels in straight lines 3 longer shadows when sun is lower 4 conclusion. Order matches the science logic.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Logical explanation plan order.","caption":"Sections should follow causes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan order</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sun → light → shadow length</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Topic</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Why shadows change</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Ordered process plan vs random sections.","caption":"Write a purpose for each section.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bread plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">mix → gas → rise</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Random</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sections ignoring causes</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y4-w08-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
